On Tue, Jun 11, 2013 at 1:35 PM, Pierre Abbat <phma@bezitopo.org> wrote:

> On Tuesday, June 11, 2013 13:05:07 .arpis. wrote:
> > {pofygau} is actually correct for one of the senses of "break" in
> > cryptography, e.g. in "MD5 is broken; use SHA2, since no one has yet
> found
> > a way to break it."
>
> OK, so what do we call a hash function?
>

{zilselfat zei mifra} (well, that's for the hash result; {te zilselfat zei
mifra} for the hash function)

{tolna'e zei mifra} for "checksum" and for "MAC", which is an unfortunate
conflation.

>
> > I second {termifyfa'i}.
>
> Sounds good, for that sense. What is the word for "key" in cryptography?
> For
> "salt"?
>

{selmipmifra} for (private) key, maybe {tolselmipmifra} for public key.

I'm not really sure for "salt"; you could make an argument for {mipmifra}
(thing that keeps the data hidden), but it would be a stretch.

Also, many of these are technical jargon and could be fu'ivla.

>
> Pierre
> --
> I believe in Yellow when I'm in Sweden and in Black when I'm in Wales.
